INT: Let's go back a little earlier, though. What did your father do before the Holocaust for a living? What was his occupation?

RK: OK. You know, Poland was very antisemitic. But in these years, like, most probably in the '20s, beginning of the 1920s, my father got a commission, a job from the government. But when Hitler came to power, the Polish people said which meant Polish people-- only Polish people are entitled to be Poles. And he was fired from this position. So we struggled. But we had an estate in a country. It was outskirt of Lódz, where I was born.

INT: What do you remember about your life before the Holocaust? What's your first memory?

RK: Well, I had a few memories because after they-- my father was fired, they had-- we had to live where our estate was, which was a place, a small place. And this is where it was a mental institution, the largest mental institution in Poland, [? Kokanuwik. ?] So we lived there, and I had to go to a Christian school. We were two Jewish children in a class. Let me tell you, it was-- it wasn't-- not nice for the Jewish children. Like when I was chosen to do-- in a theater, we made a show, a play to [? make ?] a person of this act, they were not embarrassed to tell the teacher, "Why do you give a Polish-- this is our holiday, and she is not-- she is a Jewish person. Why are you giving her this honor?" The children were very antisemitic already. So this-- being in this school was not nice for me. I was-- and another person just in the class two children, Jewish children. But then we moved to Lódz before the war. And I attended a public school, but a Jewish public school. It was such a pleasure. It was to be among your own, and you knew nobody was different. So these are my first years of my life.

INT: Do you remember your friends from the Jewish public school?

RK: I remember the friends from the Christian school.

INT: Oh.

RK: And I tell you one fact that I always speak about. I went to see my friend, a Christian. First of all, every Sunday when they left the church and I played with them, they always said, "You killed Jesus." But I said, "I wasn't live then." "It doesn't matter. The Jews killed Jesus." This was coming out from the church every-- I visited my Christian friend, and her mother was feeding a little boy. And she said, he didn't want to eat. So she said, "If you don't eat, a Jew with a long beard will come and take you away." So these are my memories of having Christian friends.

INT: Right.

RK: And I tell you, we went into the ghetto, nobody helped us.

INT: Before you went into the ghetto, what was your life like at home with your parents and your brothers and sister?

RK: Before the war?

INT: Before the war, before the ghetto.

RK: Well, I had an adult grown brother that got married. Unfortunately, he passed away just June 21, 1939. He had cancer and tuberculosis. This was a very bad, you know, situation for a young person like me. I couldn't stay home because he was contagious. I have to-- I had to live with my grandparents. Not really grandparents. It was my aunt.

INT: Were your parents religious?

RK: My mother was very Orthodox. And this is also why she died in 1940, because she wouldn't eat any treif. When we got horse meat in the ghetto, she wouldn't eat. She gave us. She had her own dishes, and she really starved. On top of it, when my brother-- one of my brothers, was still home, she used to give him some of her food.

INT: Was your father religious?

RK: My father-- you see, it's a different situation here, what means "religious." He wasn't so religious because he was a soldier. For seven years, he was in the Russian army. And then he was taken prisoner of war by the Germans. World War I we speaking about. And so, of course, it meant that he wasn't so Orthodox, but he still prayed every day, went to synagogue every day. Today, they would consider this a religious person. In these years, he was just Modern-- very. My mother, for instance, as I said, was very Orthodox. She gave her life for this.

INT: What was your favorite meal growing up?

RK: Meal?

INT: Before the Holocaust, before the ghetto?

RK: Let me tell you, I was a sickly, sick child. I had a lot of problems breathing, and I never had appetite. So they had to feed me, like, eggnogs and all kinds of things to drink because I didn't-- couldn't eat.

INT: Do you remember celebrating holidays?

RK: Yes.

INT: Which holiday-- what do you remember about that? What are your memories about--

RK: Passover. You know, my mother had to clean this house maybe a whole week. And then, when we sat down to the Seder, I only remember one thing. I woke up in bed. [LAUGHING] Because it was such a long Seder that children really couldn't fell asleep. So I remember that all of a sudden, in the morning, I am in bed.

INT: That's-- when did things start to change?

RK: In Poland?

INT: In Poland, for you.

RK: For me? You see, [PAUSES] my two brothers went to the cheders. They really were, like, forced to. And my older brother was not religious at all. [PAUSES] So it was-- I didn't change, really, till the war started. And then there is no such a question of eating treif or not eating. It's all you had.

INT: Did your father or mother want to leave Poland before the war, when things started to get bad?

RK: You see, my father in-- after World War, he was a soldier. After World War I, his brother, which was here, in Fair Lawn they lived, in Paterson. She sent him money to come. So he went to the place where they used to live. And then he had money, and he made a lot of business. So he said, what do I have to go to United States? I have the money. I can make my business right here. And this is the reason he came home and never went. And my uncle here from New Jersey never spoke to him after this.

INT: Really? That's amazing. How did you learn about the invasion of Poland? Or it just sort of-- what happened?

RK: Well, the invasion of Poland, let me tell you. I know in Lódz, all of a sudden, they said on the mics, the Germans took over everything in the government. And we heard that young people, please go to Warsaw. We have to save Warsaw. And you will have to fight. So naturally, all these young guys started on their way on foot, or maybe on a horse, to go to Warsaw. Aeroplanes were flying, German aeroplanes. And they killed them or injured them all, because it was a setup.

INT: Now, do you remember being ordered to the ghetto, your family being ordered?

RK: Yes, yes.

INT: What do you remember about hearing about that?

RK: Well, we had an order-- three days. You can take what you can carry, and you have to go to the Baluty, which was outskirt of Lódz, which were slums. Let me tell you, the bathrooms outside. Most of the time, no hot water. So this is where we had to go-- from Lódz, the town, to Baluty.

INT: How far was that?

RK: It wasn't too far. It was like-- how should I say? Like down there, downtown here from us. But the question was only that we had to find a apartment. Now, we were 350,000 Jewish people in Lódz, mostly poor Orthodox Jews. We came to the ghetto. Of course, a lot of people thought it's better to go to a different area, not to be in a big town, because Lódz was the second-largest city in Poland. So they went to different areas. So out of the 350,000 Jews, 170,000 came into the ghetto. And they had to struggle to find lodging.

INT: How did your family find lodging?

RK: They found apartment-- actually, they lived in this apartment. Because when my brother got sick and-- it's a whole story. I don't think I should tell this story because this really changed my life. He was married. And my father had to take him to a doctor, so they took him to Austria. When he came back with my brother, she closed the door, and he didn't have where to live. So my parents had to take him home.

INT: His wife closed the door? OK.

RK: Yeah. So, you know-- so I was also a child with certain illnesses, old breathing problems. So they had to send me to my aunt to live because my brother was contagious. He had tuberculosis.

INT: Now, the apartment that you lived in, in the ghetto, how big was that?

RK: I remember it had a bedroom and a kitchen.

INT: And how many people were in this apartment?

RK: Well, my father, my mother, my sister, and two brothers. And my oldest brother, because she closed the door, my mother had to take him home. And my brother died June 21, 1939 in our apartment.

INT: What was life like for you in the ghetto? Were you working or trying-- going to school? What were you doing?

RK: I went to school in the ghetto. Well, the ghetto opened in 1939. And there were all kinds of different periods of how it-- what it was with us. First, we had money, so you-- we bought the food. My father was alive with my mother, and I had the two brothers. And my third brother, as I told you, was so sick, and he lived with us. So as long we-- my father had money, we had food. And then other people were in the same condition. So when the ghetto-- we came to the ghetto, and the Alteste was Chaim Rumkowski was in charge of the ghetto, Lódz Ghetto. So they did-- they did-- they did-- they had to open up like a government. So we have all kinds of department of health, department of sanitation. Everything was set up like a government. And Chaim Rumkowski was in charge. And he had to do what the Germans told him to do. So the Germans were not in charge of the ghetto, but they were in charge of Rumkowski. And he did whatever they-- he's supposed to do. Now, the history still doesn't reconcile whether he did all right or he didn't all right. The question is, still, we survived, many of us. But other people are very harsh on him. A matter of fact, when he arrived with his family to Auschwitz, the kapos from the ghetto killed him.

INT: What did Rykowski do that people were so against?

RK: Rumkowski? You know what? Every time they wanted people, we didn't know where the people go. Let me tell you. No radio, no papers, no nothing. We were in the dark, because we know that people were sent away. We didn't know where. So we don't know about this. But Rumkowski most probably knew because he had orders. And every time, whatever they wanted, he gave. So we still had a ghetto. [PAUSES] Well, let me tell you, when they-- when they deported him, he was killed brutally with the whole family. And believe me, they didn't deserve it, because I worked with his sister-in-law. And she was also killed. And she worked with me in the children's resort. We were about 600 children, and she worked with me, because we used to do-- we used to collect from everybody a spoon of flour or a spoon of sugar to give it to the children that were in the hospital of the children that was very sick.

INT: So you were working with them?

RK: I worked with Helena Rumkowski, and they killed her, too. So you know what? It wasn't always the right thing to do.

INT: Now, you said you went to school in the ghetto?

RK: Yes.

INT: Now--

RK: I went to-- the school was not within Lódz. It was an outskirt, Marysin.

INT: And you were allowed to go out of the ghetto to that?

RK: This wasn't-- this was a part of the ghetto, with the cemetery. Was also a part of the ghetto.

INT: Now, did you-- how about food when you were in the ghetto? What happened?

RK: Well, in the beginning, my father said-- had money, so we bought food. And then it was the end of it. They closed the ghetto, and we could in-- not go in or out, with barbed wire. The only good part of it, it was-- it was not electric barbed wire, just a regular barbed wire. So nobody got killed unless they smuggled themselves out of the ghetto.

INT: Was there a lot of smuggling?

RK: No.

INT: Now, you said they closed the ghetto. Do you remember what year that was or about?

RK: I would say it was the end of '39.

INT: Yeah. Now, when-- [PAUSES] well, the war started September 1, 1939. So it had to be the end, I guess. Now, when you were in the ghetto, they started to send people out of the ghetto?

RK: Not yet.

INT: Not yet? When did that start?

RK: This started if you-- we were in the ghetto maybe a year or two.

INT: Right.

RK: We didn't know about where the people go. I only know my middle brother, [? Leon, ?] volunteered in 1940 to go to Germany to work.

INT: And he was killed.

RK: So 400 young guys-- he was 17-- young guys went to Germany. And what did they do? They build the Strasse nach Berlin.

From which one was it? [UNCLEAR] Anyway, this what he did. And then, they sent him home. But he never re-entered the ghetto. We didn't know about it. They sent him, and he was in another concentration camp in Poland. And he lived through till almost the end of the war. Then they liquidated this concentration camp, and they sent him to Auschwitz. And this what their friends tell-- told me. It was soon before the Russian liberated Auschwitz. And what happened was, he did something. He stole a bread from a pushcart. And they killed him. And this was very close that Auschwitz was liberated by the Russians.

INT: Now, when you were in the ghetto, your parents both died when you were in the ghetto.

RK: My both parents had the honor die in a bed.

INT: Right.

RK: My friend's parents didn't. They were shot. They were gassed. But my both parents, by coincident, got sick. My father got sick already in 1939. As soon as we were, we-- just the ghetto didn't even open. And he died in a hospital. My mother died of starvation. But when we called to the health department, and they said, "Give me symptoms." So I said, "High temperature, and she feels very sick." "Typhoid fever." So they said typhoid fever. And as soon as they said it, the same night-- I remember like it was now-- they came, and they locked us up. And they said, "Eintritt verboten." "Entrance not allowed." And they put a big sign of it. And the same night, there was embarkation. Now, this was March, and it was snowing and bitter cold. And the people that were taken away at night cried and prayed, prayed to God they should-- they should let them stay. But they took them away in the night. And we figured that this is not to live.

INT: So being--

RK: But because we were "Eintritt verboten," typhoid fever, which we didn't have, saved our lives. This was all the time for something-- something happened, beyond us doing it, saved our lives.

INT: Now, I think you told me that there was a time in the ghetto that you had to hide from the Germans.

RK: Well--

INT: Could you tell us about that?

RK: Yes. They also-- yes, this hiding. You see, they helped-- schools' students, when they interview me, ask, what's the worst thing? So I tell you what the worst thing was. For me, it was hunger, cold. We were so cold. These winters were so-- we didn't have enough coal. And then fear. We were always afraid. We were always hiding in the ghetto because the Germans, they could enter anytime they wanted. And we were-- I tell you something. If you had a baby in the hiding, it was so dangerous for this baby because they lacked-- they closed their mouths, and some didn't even survive. That's how bad it was, the hiding.

INT: Where would you hide?

RK: Closets. I tell you, I lived through one of the embarkation. This was the last. They took these people to Auschwitz. We didn't know. I was just on the way from my friend that I saw the Germans came back, because they usually, by 5 o'clock, they were gone. But this particular embarkation, all of a sudden, we see 5 o'clock they bad-- back. And I'm in my friend's house. So my friend's mother pleaded with me, stay. I said, no, I have to go to my sister. My sister was with my friend because have-- my friend's parents were taken away. So she lived with us. So I said, no, I have to go to my sister. I can't leave her. So of course, I was marching with the whole group of people. The Germans were standing. Matter of fact, I went over to the German guy, and I said to him, can you-- can I go for my sister and I come back? So he said, I send somebody. But after he did it, he really sended. But she didn't want to come out, because she didn't trust. It was-- that's how she survived. But I ran away. So when the German wasn't looking, I ran, and a-- and a police from the ghetto was after me. And I said, what do you care? Why don't you let me go? And finally, he let me go. So I went up. And I tell you, it was very quiet. Now, the steps were from wood, and you wouldn't believe they make a noise. And here, I'm sneaking in to the-- to the top of the-- I forgot just which. And when I opened the door, people were hiding there, and they were fuming. "Get out of here! Because of you, they discover us." So I had to leave. But the doors were all chopped up. So I went into the first apartment available, and I took a sweater, and I took a plate and a spoon to prepare to go. And I was hiding there in the closet-- I don't know whose apartment it was-- until they left. So they took the people, and we don't know where they send them. But I survived. My sister also survived.

INT: It must have been really difficult for you and your sister when your parents died. Did you just-- did you stay in the apartment? Did you live with someone else, or what did you do?

RK: No, we had to leave because the house that we lived in was wood. One day, we woke up, and they took the house apart, the wall, for-- because they need-- we used-- I told you, we didn't have heat. We were dying of the cold in the winter. So they took away some of the house. So they gave us another apartment. So we shared with two girls that their parents were taken away.

INT: Now, how long were you in the ghetto, the Lódz Ghetto? From when you were there, when did you leave the ghetto?

RK: Well, the ghetto-- first of all, we're speaking about Lódz. They gave us permission that we can take three days. We can take whatever we can carry in three days to go to the Baluty. So we got an apartment there. And then, you know, we stayed there till we went to Auschwitz.

INT: And tell me about that. How did that-- did they select you to go to Auschwitz, or how did that happen?

RK: Well, we were in the ghetto a few years. Must have been till 1942. [PAUSES] And of course, the Russians were approaching, so they closed the ghetto. And they said we can carry whatever we can to a center. So of course, we took all the bedding, pots, and pans, so make it easy for the Germans because everything went to Germany. We didn't go because we were sent to Auschwitz.

INT: I assume you went on a train to Auschwitz with your sister.

RK: Oh, yes. So first I was-- we were hiding in a-- it was a meadow, and it was already in the Germans, where they [? went. ?] So my cousins came, because we all lived in Lódz. And they decided we should go, and they wanted us to go, too. So my sister, myself, and my friend that lived with me, because my brother wasn't there anymore. Now, my brother was-- this was 1942, and they decided that people go to work, only healthy, young people. I was sick. I had temperature, so I was not allowed to go. They accumulated 10,000, only healthy, young people. And they were killed within three days. And they didn't take them to Germany. They were killed in one of these killing places in Poland.

INT: When you went to Auschwitz, was your sister with you?

RK: Yes.

INT: And--

RK: And all my cousins.

INT: And all your cousins? How many cousins were with you?

RK: Was about-- let's see. At least seven or eight, and my aunt.

INT: You-- was the train trip-- do you remember the train trip?

RK: I tell you, the train to Auschwitz, we were-- it was hot. It was summer. So the only openings was that the wagons had the wood was not closed. So in between was a opening. So this is where I-- we could breathe because the air came in. We were about 80 girls going. No, it's not only girls. 80 people going to Auschwitz.

INT: And when you got there, what happened?

RK: To Auschwitz?

INT: To Auschwitz.

RK: OK, first, you know, they marched us. I tell you, I saw people with wire. And one group of people, women, was like very dark, charcoaled. I couldn't understand. You see, they committed suicide. So they touched the wire, and they were elected. And if you touch the person's hand, you get the same. So a whole line of women were like this, charcoaled. I went over to the German soldier, and I said, He said-- I said, who are these people? He said, They are crazy, he said. All these people, the women, committed suicide. Now also, they were asking-- the rest of the people were asking us, please-- in a few languages, in Polish, Yiddish, and German-- give us what you have. You don't need it. Give us all this. Nobody gave anything. We didn't believe. I went over. So the Germans said, they are crazy. So I thought they were crazy because they looked crazy-- shaved. We still had hair.

INT: Were you scared?

RK: I tell you one thing-- if I would have a pill, I would have committed suicide at this point. This point, I already saw that it's not a normal place.

INT: Yeah. How long were you in Auschwitz?

RK: Not long. About two weeks.

INT: Right. And what happened then? Where did you go from there?

RK: First of all, let me tell you. In Auschwitz, [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] we didn't know. But the people were sent there before, so some of the people that we came had family there. So the girls came over and say, you see this smoke? These are the people you came with. Of course, we were in a shock-- we didn't-- because we didn't know. So they took us-- first of all, there were baths, and there were dishes, rings, watches, anything-- earrings. All this prepared, whatever we had, we had to put it in the right place. All this went to Germany, everything what we had. And then they just shaved us. And then they gave us a pajama top and a skirt. Nothing else. And some-- they took away our shoes. They just left us with wooden shoes.

I used to recognize my sister by the skirt because women without hair looked completely different. It wasn't normal. None of our lives was-- at this part was normal.

INT: You were in Auschwitz for two weeks, right?

RK: Yes.

INT: And did you work there or just--

RK: First of all, I wasn't feeling well. This was another story. And then, nobody worked. Nobody. We just got our rations. When we came, the first food was a bowl with soup for five girls-- no spoon, nothing. Just everybody gave a sip, and the other girl took a sip. Now, I have all my cousins with me, so we didn't cheat. Because, you know, you could only take and give it to your next cousin. After this, they gave us a lodging, a big place with five on the bottom and five on top. Wood. And this is where we were, just with a blanket. This was the place we were sleeping. And of course, every day we had to be counted.

INT: Now, you said you were there for two weeks.

RK: Yeah.

INT: Where did-- how did you-- where did you go next? Where did you go from there?

RK: After this, they sent us to a meadow, "eine grüne Wiese."

INT: Could you explain that? Yeah.

RK: "Grüne Wiese." This was a place, a meadow with grass, green.

INT: Mm-hmm. Right.

RK: This is where they send us. And I tell you, we were so cold. We were freezing. This is how we had to spend this night. You know, we had to sit next to my sister, both of us, so our bodies were warmed the next body. And in the morning, they let us take a shower. And then they opened the windows and the doors to dry up. And then they gave us a package. First, a dress and shoes, nothing else. So it was random. So what happened? Everybody got a dress. My sister got with long sleeve a long dress. I got-- So she said, you see what they gave me? So she complained. I got a short dress, short sleeves. And I paid the price for this. And then, after the shower, they opened the windows and the doors, and let us dry up, and they gave us a package. It was some bread and marmalade. And we got a train, and we were sent away.

INT: Were you scared of where you were going to be sent?

RK: We didn't know where we going. No, we didn't know about Auschwitz, that we were-- we were completely-- like, because you had no radio, no newspaper, no nothing.

INT: Right.

RK: So we didn't know anything, what's going on in the world.

INT: So when they sent you out of Auschwitz, where did-- after you left there, where did you go?

RK: To Germany. This was Hambühren bei Hannover, close to Hanover, which is a big town. You know, the Germans said after the war, we didn't know. What do you mean, you didn't know? The people close to the camps didn't know? You know, when we marched, instead of give us some water, they threw-- the kids are throwing stones at us. Nobody gave us something to drink.

INT: Now, you said you went to Hambühren?

RK: Hambühren bei Hannover.

INT: Do you know how to spell that, or?

RK: Hambühren is H-A-B-B-I-E-R-N.

INT: And that was by Hanover, Germany?

RK: Yes.

INT: And what did you do there?

RK: Building huts. So you know what? This was September. It was so cold. I was naked-- naked arms in a short dress. I was freezing, and I was sick. And we were had to carry to-- to do the huts. You know, pre-existing because they took just some cement, you know, one of these big-- and they built-- we built a house, a hut.

INT: How many of you were there building?

RK: Well, we were-- from our town, we were 450 girls. And after a while, I couldn't take it anymore. So I said to my sister, listen, I am going to report I'm sick. If I die, I die. I can't go on. I'm freezing, and I'm sick. So you see, this is another time that you don't count how you survive. So I was taken. All of a sudden, I was taking to a place with a bed and had sheets. And I-- a sick bed. It was such a surprise where I'm going. So you know what? After I recovered, [PAUSES] they discharged me. Now, I was out with a few girls that were discharged.

INT: And this was in the same-- by this camp?

RK: Yes.

INT: Yeah.

RK: Hambühren. Then, one of the-- he was an officer, came. And we were transferred to another camp.

INT: Was your sister still with you?

RK: Yeah.

